# Everything on this list is a triaged, documented deferral rather than a
# silent suppression. A new or undocumented vulnerability, secret or
# misconfiguration still fails the build. The value of the list is that what
# is on it can be read and disagreed with; an entry with no reasoning under
# it does not belong here.

# --- Both containers run as root ---
# AVD-DS-0002, raised against backend/Dockerfile and frontend/Dockerfile.
# Real, worth fixing, and not fixed - so it is written down rather than
# quietly dropped from the scanner's severity range.
#
# What makes it more than adding one USER line: the backend writes to bind
# mounts of host directories (./backend/data holds the SQLite databases, the
# vector index and backups). A bind mount keeps the host directory's
# ownership, so a non-root user inside the container has to match a uid that
# only the operator's machine knows. Adding USER without solving that gives a
# container that cannot write its own database on first boot, and solving it
# properly still leaves anyone already running v0.1.x with a data directory
# their upgraded container can no longer write. That is a change carrying an
# upgrade note for existing deployments, not a build fix, so it wants its own
# release rather than a quiet edit. Tracked in ROADMAP.md.
#
# What bounds it meanwhile, from docker-compose.yml: neither container is
# privileged, neither adds capabilities, and nothing outside this project
# directory is mounted - the corpus, the operator docs and the eval seed are
# all read-only. Root here is root inside a container whose only writable
# mount is its own data directory. That is a weaker boundary than a non-root
# user, which is why this is a deferral and not an acceptance.
AVD-DS-0002
